A||N||3||DaveL||Compressor Oil||||5||
Z||000000||DaveL||04-26-2001||03:54 PM||||I am getting ready to put in a brand new A.C. compressor for my 87 SE 2.5, along with a new dryer and orifice. How much oil should go in this compressor for new installation? <P>The unit did not work for the first 2 years I owned it, then I had it converted to r-134 and a new dryer and orifice out in it. It worked fine for a week or so, then the old compressor siezed up. Florida is a hot place, so I neeeeeeeeeeeed A.C.<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||Phil||04-26-2001||05:35 PM||||A new compressor should already be filled with oil, but you should find out what kind of oil is in it. If it's R12 mineral drain it out and refill with 8 oz. of R134A compatable (pag or ester)||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||04-26-2001||07:08 PM||||A new V5 compressor Will Not come filled with oil. This is because there are 3 kinds of oil choices, partly depending on which refrigerant is to be installed.<P>I don't have my specs for the AC handy.... it's a couple ounces, not 8. It has to be the same oil used for the previous conversion.<P>You need to be carefull how much oil is in the system. V5 will not like the system running with too much oil. These compressors DO NOT operate the same way as the Harrison A-6 used on older Fiero 4 cyls and the V6's.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 04-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Phil||04-26-2001||07:28 PM||||I was just relating what the instructions that came with my new compressor stated.||||reg||1
